============================================================================================
w140c — where the page ceiling actually is, and what it does to the doctrine
============================================================================================

T1. WHERE IS THE CEILING?
  PASS  T1  the server caps a page at exactly 200 rows
          requested   150 ->  150 rows
          requested   199 ->  199 rows
          requested   200 ->  200 rows
          requested   201 ->  200 rows
          requested   500 ->  200 rows
          requested  1000 ->  200 rows
          true total (paginated) = 201
          the ceiling sits between 201 and 200: asking for 201 already truncates.

T2. THE DOCTRINE'S OWN TEST, AT THE PAGE SIZE THE WORKSPACE STANDARDISED ON
  PASS  T2  at PAGE=500 the read IS truncated and `len(rows) >= PAGE` CANNOT fire
          rows returned 200, true total 201 -> truncated by 1 row(s)
          `200 >= 500` is False -> the guard reports NOT TRUNCATED
          raising PAGE past the ceiling disables the detector instead of widening the read.

T3. DOES THE CLI SURFACE THE TOKEN THE SERVER RETURNS?
  PASS  T3  the server returns a next_page_token at page 500 and the CLI never prints it
          kagglesdk single page at 500: 200 rows, next_page_token present = True
          CLI at 500: token surfaced on stdout/stderr = None
          so `w23b_sendqueue.py`'s `if len(rows) >= PAGE or tok:` -- the ONE site that thought
          to check a token -- reads tok=None and passes a truncated list too.

T4. CAN THE CERTIFIED SCRIPTS READ A COMPLETE LIST?
  PASS  T4  every deadline-day script now reads a COMPLETE list
          w84a_pickargmax.py         paginated=True  raw page sizes=[200]  complete: True
          w26g_send.py               paginated=True  raw page sizes=-  complete: True
          w23b_sendqueue.py          paginated=True  raw page sizes=-  complete: True
          w55a_unpriced.py           paginated=True  raw page sizes=-  complete: True
          w54a_vetoexpiry.py         paginated=True  raw page sizes=-  complete: True
          w85c_slotguard.py          paginated=True  raw page sizes=-  complete: True
          w74b_clickstaleguard.py    paginated=True  raw page sizes=-  complete: True
          7/7 read the full list.
  PASS  T4-ctl  the pre-w140 corpus is condemned by the same predicate
          w84a_pickargmax.py         PAGE=500  above the 200 ceiling -> `len(rows) >= 500` unfireable
          w26g_send.py               PAGE=500  above the 200 ceiling -> `len(rows) >= 500` unfireable
          w23b_sendqueue.py          PAGE=500  above the 200 ceiling -> `len(rows) >= 500` unfireable
          w55a_unpriced.py           PAGE=500  above the 200 ceiling -> `len(rows) >= 500` unfireable
          w54a_vetoexpiry.py         PAGE=500  above the 200 ceiling -> `len(rows) >= 500` unfireable
          w85c_slotguard.py          PAGE=500  above the 200 ceiling -> `len(rows) >= 500` unfireable
          w74b_clickstaleguard.py    PAGE=500  above the 200 ceiling -> `len(rows) >= 500` unfireable
          7/7 were undetectably truncated while G4 called them protected.

T5. CONTROL — IS THE IDIOM ITSELF BROKEN, OR ONLY BROKEN ABOVE THE CEILING?
  PASS  T5  BELOW the ceiling the same `len(rows) >= PAGE` idiom fires correctly
          requested 150 -> 150 rows; `150 >= 150` is True -> TRUNCATION DETECTED
          so the idiom is sound and the CAP is what disables it. Without this control,
          'the check does not work' and 'the check is switched off by the cap' look the same.

T6. WHAT DOES WORK — three independent reads of the same total
  PASS  T6  pagination and an outside witness agree, and neither depends on a page size
          paginated read (follow next_page_token): 201 over 2 pages
          public leaderboard SubmissionCount column: 201
          ⟹ THE REMEDY IS NOT A BIGGER NUMBER. Follow the token, or compare the length against
            a total obtained some other way (`num_total`, or the board's own SubmissionCount).

